# Laya for Linux x86_64 with AMD XDNA2

This prebuilt package contains Laya, its shared GEMM library, the matching
XDNA2 NPU kernel, and license notices. It reads Laya GGUF models directly.

Built on: @BUILD_HOST@. CPU native optimization: @GGML_NATIVE@.

## Requirements

- Linux x86_64 with AVX2 and an AMD XDNA2 NPU. Tested on Ryzen AI 9 HX370.
- glibc providing GLIBC_@GLIBC_REQUIRED@ or later.
- libstdc++ providing GLIBCXX_@GLIBCXX_REQUIRED@ and CXXABI_@CXXABI_REQUIRED@.
- Installed XRT NPU runtime (`libxrt_coreutil.so.2`) with its AMD XDNA plugin.
  The integration was tested with XRT 2.21.75.
- Loaded amdxdna driver, compatible firmware and access to `/dev/accel/accel0`.
- A Laya GGUF model, downloaded separately, and sufficient memory/memlock
  allowance for the original weights and BF16 NPU buffers.

XRT, its plugin, the driver, glibc and libstdc++ are system dependencies.
They are not included in this archive. Build-machine ABI requirements are
recorded from the bundled ELF files in `BUILD-INFO.txt`; this package does
not promise compatibility with older Ubuntu releases.

## Run after extraction

```bash
tar -xzf @PACKAGE_NAME@.tar.gz
cd @PACKAGE_NAME@
sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
./@INSTALL_BINDIR@/laya help
./@INSTALL_BINDIR@/laya decide /path/to/laya_english_q8_0.gguf \
  --preset email --device amdxdna --threads 8 --json
```

`@INSTALL_BINDIR@/laya` resolves `@INSTALL_LIBDIR@/libgemm.so` and
`@INSTALL_DATADIR@/laya/amdxdna/mm.xclbin` relative to its own location. The complete
directory can be moved; it runs without `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` or a FastFlowLM
checkout. Keep `bin`, `lib` and `share` together.

To install in `/opt/laya` after extraction:

```bash
sudo mkdir -p /opt/laya
sudo cp -a . /opt/laya/
/opt/laya/@INSTALL_BINDIR@/laya help
```

Verify device detection with `xrt-smi examine`. Set
`GGMLC_AMDXDNA_TRACE=1` when running Laya to log each forward; a positive
`npu_matmuls` count records successfully completed NPU GEMMs. Other operators
run on CPU. An explicit `GGMLC_AMDXDNA_XCLBIN` overrides the bundled kernel.

## Precision and notices

The NPU backend is experimental and uses BF16. Model probabilities and
decisions can differ from CPU inference; validate your workload before use.

Bundled NPU components retain their original proprietary binary terms in
`@INSTALL_DATADIR@/laya/amdxdna/licenses/FastFlowLM-TERMS.md`. Other license notices and
provenance are under `@INSTALL_DATADIR@/laya/amdxdna` and `@INSTALL_DATADIR@/laya/licenses`.

GGUF models: https://huggingface.co/mys/laya-GGUF
